    menu "MAX31856 Thermocouple Channels (via J6)"
        config KILNCTL_THERMO_CS0_IO
            int "Channel 0 ~CS GPIO"
            default 14
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_THERMO_CS1_IO
            int "Channel 1 ~CS GPIO"
            default 17
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_THERMO_CS2_IO
            int "Channel 2 ~CS GPIO"
            default 18
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_THERMO_FAULT0_IO
            int "Channel 0 ~FAULT GPIO"
            default 38
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_THERMO_FAULT1_IO
            int "Channel 1 ~FAULT GPIO"
            default 47
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_THERMO_FAULT2_IO
            int "Channel 2 ~FAULT GPIO"
            default 48
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_THERMO_SPI_CLOCK_HZ
            int "MAX31856 SPI clock (Hz)"
            default 4000000
            range 100000 4000000
            help
                4 MHz is a CEILING, not just a default -- the range above
                will not let you raise it, and MAX31856.c repeats the limit
                as a _Static_assert in case sdkconfig is hand-edited.

                The part itself is rated to 5 MHz, so the cap is not the
                silicon. It is the SimFW bench fixture: its PIO/DMA MAX31856
                slave emulation must present the first response byte before
                the master's MISO sample point, which is one SCLK period
                minus setup -- about 250 ns at 4 MHz, tightening to about
                200 ns at 5 MHz. The implemented DMA-fed path takes about
                150-215 ns at the RP2040's stock 125 MHz sysclk. That has
                margin at 4 MHz and largely none at 5 MHz. See
                firmware/SimFW/docs/SPI_ACCESS_AUDIT.md section 9 for the
                derivation.

                Missing that deadline does not look like a fault. The whole
                burst shifts by one byte position and returns plausible
                temperatures that are wrong, which is why this is enforced
                rather than merely commented.

                This does not constrain the ILI9488, which is a separate
                device on the same bus with its own clock
                (KILNCTL_DISPLAY_SPI_CLOCK_HZ) and is unaffected by this cap.

                The signals also cross a ribbon cable to the daughterboard,
                so back this off further if reads come back corrupted.

        config KILNCTL_THERMO_DEFAULT_AUTO_CONVERT
            bool "Start channels in automatic conversion mode"
            default y
            help
                Automatic mode converts continuously (~100 ms with one
                sample averaged) and asserts ~DRDY each time. With this off
                the channels come up idle and each reading needs an explicit
                one-shot.
    endmenu

    menu "SX1509 I/O Expander (relays + digital I/O + display control)"
        config KILNCTL_SX1509_I2C_ADDR
            hex "SX1509 I2C address"
            default 0x3E
            help
                ADDR1/ADDR0 both strapped to GND on this board, giving 0x3E.
                The part also answers at 0x3F/0x70/0x71 when strapped
                differently; the running firmware can be re-targeted from the
                PC without reflashing.

        config KILNCTL_SX1509_IRQ_IO
            int "SX1509 ~INT GPIO"
            default 7
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_SX1509_RESET_IO
            int "SX1509 ~RESET GPIO"
            default 10
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_SX1509_RELAYS_OFF_ON_LINK_LOSS
            bool "Drop all relays when the PC link goes away"
            default y
            help
                A kiln element left on by a crashed or unplugged controller
                is the failure this exists to prevent. Turn it off only for
                bench work where the board is expected to run unattached.

        config KILNCTL_PC_LINK_LOSS_ASSERTS_FAULT
            bool "Assert the isolated fault line when the PC link goes away"
            default n
            help
                Whether losing the PC control link also asserts the isolated
                fault line to the safety processor, which trips it.

                Off by default, deliberately. The PC link is a debug and
                control channel for the MCP tooling, not something the kiln
                needs in order to run: this board has its own LCD and web UI
                and is expected to fire without a host attached. With this on,
                a board sitting on the bench with nothing plugged into it
                asserts the fault line five seconds after boot, trips the
                safety processor with S6a, and stays tripped -- the trip
                latches, so it survives the link coming back. That is what a
                standalone board did before this option existed, and it made
                the panel show a permanent fault for no hazard.

                Turn it on for a setup where the PC genuinely is the operator
                interface and its absence really does mean the kiln is
                unattended. The relay drop above is a separate switch and is
                still on by default either way.
    endmenu

    menu "ILI9488 Display (BIGTREETECH TFT35 SPI V2.1, J2)"
        choice KILNCTL_DISPLAY_PANEL
            prompt "Panel driven by panel_spi.c"
            default KILNCTL_DISPLAY_PANEL_ILI9488
            help
                DISPLAY_ST7796_PLAN.md Sec.6 Step 3 / Sec.12 Phase 3:
                Kconfig-only panel selection, no auto-detection yet -- that
                is Phase 4, and it is blocked on Sec.4's bench-recorded
                RDDID bytes, which do not exist. ILI9488 (the BIGTREETECH
                TFT35 actually wired to J2 today) stays the default; picking
                ST7796 selects the LCDWIKI/Elecrow MSP4031 driver, which has
                NEVER RUN ON HARDWARE -- see the STOP block at the top of
                the plan doc before that module goes anywhere near J2.
                There is deliberately no "auto" choice here: this phase
                explicitly does not implement detection, and an auto option
                that silently fell back to one fixed panel would misrepresent
                that as a real choice.

            config KILNCTL_DISPLAY_PANEL_ILI9488
                bool "ILI9488 (BIGTREETECH TFT35 SPI V2.1)"

            config KILNCTL_DISPLAY_PANEL_ST7796
