        config KILNCTL_DISPLAY_SWAP_DC_RESET
            bool "Take the schematic's LCD_IORQ/LCD_Reset naming literally"
            default n
            help
                The main board calls J2 pin 1 LCD_IORQ and pin 4 LCD_Reset,
                but every available pinout for the BIGTREETECH TFT35 SPI
                module says pin 1 is the touch controller's interrupt and
                pin 4 is RS (data/command), with no panel reset brought out.
                Off (the default) follows the module: data/command on
                expander IO15 (J2 pin 4). Turn this on to use the
                schematic's naming instead: data/command on IO14 (pin 1).
                If the panel shows nothing but its ID reads back correctly,
                this is the first thing to flip.

        config KILNCTL_DISPLAY_ROTATION
            int "Startup rotation (0-3)"
            default 1
            range 0 3
            help
                0/2 are portrait 320x480, 1/3 landscape 480x320.

        config KILNCTL_DISPLAY_DC_RESET_DIRECT_GPIO
            bool "Drive D/C and ~RESET from ESP32 GPIOs directly (bench wiring)"
            default n
            help
                Bench-wiring override for bring-up when the SX1509 expander
                isn't available or isn't trusted yet: instead of D/C and
                ~RESET going through the expander (IO14/IO15, the normal main
                board wiring -- see KILNCTL_DISPLAY_SWAP_DC_RESET), drive them
                from two bare ESP32 GPIOs, same as ~CS already is. With this
                on, the display no longer needs the expander at all -- it will
                come up even if the SX1509 is dead or unwired, unlike the
                normal (off) wiring where the expander is required just to
                send a single command. Off (the default) matches the main
                board as designed. All other display connections (SPI,
                ~CS, power) are unaffected either way.

        config KILNCTL_DISPLAY_DC_GPIO
            int "D/C GPIO (direct-wired bench mode)"
            default 0
            range 0 48
            depends on KILNCTL_DISPLAY_DC_RESET_DIRECT_GPIO

        config KILNCTL_DISPLAY_RESET_GPIO
            int "~RESET GPIO (direct-wired bench mode)"
            default 1
            range 0 48
            depends on KILNCTL_DISPLAY_DC_RESET_DIRECT_GPIO
    endmenu

    menu "NS2009 Touch Controller (on the TFT35 SPI panel, J2)"
        config KILNCTL_TOUCH_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS
            int "Screen auto-blank timeout (ms, 0 = disabled)"
            default 0
            range 0 3600000
            help
                How long the panel goes with no touch -- real, from the
                NS2009, or synthetic, injected over the UART bridge's
                TOUCH_CMD_INJECT -- before screen_idle_task paints the frame
                solid black (ILI9488_clear). This board has no backlight
                control line, so that -- not ILI9488_set_power(false), which
                blanks to a bright WHITE page on this panel -- is the closest
                to "dark" firmware can manage; the backlight itself stays
                lit. Saving the panel's useful life is still the point. A
                touch of either kind wakes it again immediately (screen_on
                goes back to true; repainting real content is the PC/
                dashboard's job, same as any other redraw).

                Set to 0 to disable auto-blank entirely -- the screen simply
                stays on (2026-08-18: made the default per user request).
                screen_idle_task still polls the touch controller and still
                services injected touches harmlessly when disabled; it just
                never blanks. Touch activity tracking (TOUCH_CMD_GET_STATE's
                idle_ms) keeps working either way.

        config KILNCTL_TOUCH_Z1_MAX_THRESHOLD
            int "Touch pressure threshold (raw NS2009 Z1 ADC counts, 0-4095)"
            default 200
            range 0 4095
            help
                NS2009_read treats a Z1 reading above this as "pressed".
                The NS2009 datasheet's typical application (and this
                driver's original assumption) has a touch drive Z1 *low*,
                with an untouched panel floating near full scale -- that
                does NOT hold on this board revision. Bench testing
                2026-08-19 (device log, NS2009's raw_x/raw_y/z1 diagnostic)
                found the opposite: Z1 floats at 0-30 with nothing touching
                the panel, and a real firm press drove it to 1047 -- either
                a wiring difference (see the SDA/SCL-swap question in
                docs/HARDWARE.md) or this panel's Z1 behaves differently in
                the "short driver, auto power-down" mode this driver uses.
                The 200 default sits well above the observed idle-noise
                ceiling (30) and well below the one observed real press
                (1047), with margin on both sides since only one bench
                sample of a real press exists so far. If touches are missed
                or the panel reports phantom presses with nothing touching
                it, this is the first thing to tune, the same way
                KILNCTL_DISPLAY_SWAP_DC_RESET is the first thing to flip for
                a blank panel.
    endmenu

    menu "LVGL Touchscreen UI (TODO.md section 10)"
        config KILNCTL_LVGL_BUF_ROWS
            int "LVGL draw buffer height (panel rows)"
            default 40
            range 4 480
            help
                Each of LVGL's two draw buffers is this many rows tall (at
                the panel's current rotated width) x 2 bytes/pixel (RGB565),
                allocated in PSRAM (see TODO.md 9.1a's 2026-08-17 reversal).
                Smaller means more, smaller flush calls (finer partial
                redraws, more SPI transaction overhead); larger means fewer,
                bigger flushes. 40 rows at 480px wide is 2 x 38.4 KB -- a
                rounding error against 8 MB of PSRAM, so raise this freely if
                flush overhead ever shows up as sluggish.

        config KILNCTL_TOUCH_CAL_SWAP_XY
            bool "Touch: swap raw X/Y axes before mapping to pixels"
            default n
            help
                Resistive touch panels have no fixed relationship between
                their raw X/Y axes and the panel's rotated pixel axes -- it
                depends on how the film is oriented under the glass, which is
                not documented for this module. Like
                KILNCTL_DISPLAY_SWAP_DC_RESET and KILNCTL_TOUCH_Z1_MAX_THRESHOLD,
                this is a bench-calibration knob, not a datasheet value: if
                touches land rotated 90 degrees from where they should,
                this is the first thing to flip.

        config KILNCTL_TOUCH_CAL_INVERT_X
            bool "Touch: invert raw X axis"
            default n

        config KILNCTL_TOUCH_CAL_INVERT_Y
            bool "Touch: invert raw Y axis"
            default n
    endmenu

    menu "Safety Processor Link (opto-isolated UART to the RP2040)"
        config KILNCTL_SAFETY_TX_IO
            int "Safety UART TX GPIO"
            default 5
            range 0 48
            help
                Drives U2's optocoupler LED through R12 (390R) via net
                DataToSafty, whose collector is the Pico's RX (GP5, PicoRx).

        config KILNCTL_SAFETY_RX_IO
            int "Safety UART RX GPIO"
            default 4
            range 0 48
            help
                U3's collector, net DataFromSafty (the Pico's TX, GP4,
                drives U3's LED through R7). R15 (1k to 3.3V_Main) is fitted
                externally on this net; the internal pull-up is belt-and-braces.

        config KILNCTL_SAFETY_FAULT_IO
            int "Isolated fault line GPIO (output)"
            default 6
            range 0 48
            help
                Output, not input: driving it high lights U1's LED and pulls
                the safety processor's mainFault input low.

        config KILNCTL_SAFETY_BAUD_RATE
            int "Safety link baud rate"
            default 230400
            help
                Must stay equal to SaftyFW's UART_OWNER_BAUD_RATE
                (src/tasks/uart_owner.c) AND to the bootloader's recovery-mode
                uart_init (bootloader/main.c) -- all three hardcode it, there
                is no negotiation. A mismatch does not degrade, it produces
                framing errors and nothing else.

                HISTORY, because the old number here was widely quoted and is
                now wrong. Until 2026-08-25 this was 9600, and that ceiling
                was a property of the TCMT1109 optocouplers, not of either
                firmware. Measured 2026-08-23 across the opto pair:

                  115200 -- zero frames received, ever
                   57600 -- zero frames received, ever
                   38400 -- about 80% received (53 of 66), errors climbing
                   19200 -- clean over a short window, but over a longer one
                            received lagged sent (107 of 118, then 117 of
                            134): about 10% of frames still lost
                    9600 -- received tracks sent one for one over minutes

                Those optocouplers were replaced on 2026-08-25 by a single
                ADuM1201WT digital isolator (U6, hardware/mainBoard/
                SaftyProcessor.kicad_sch), which is non-inverting, has one
                unidirectional channel per direction, and is rated far past
                anything this UART needs. The inversions both firmwares used
                to apply to cancel the optos' own inversion were removed in
                the same change -- see safety_link.c's comment at
                uart_owner_init, and uart_owner.c's at uart_owner_init.

                MEASURED SWEEP RESULTS, against U6, 60 seconds continuous per
                rate, both sides rebuilt and reflashed for each rate. A rate
                passes only if NONE of these four counters gain a single count
                during the window -- crc/framing errors, length mismatch, crc
                mismatch, resync -- AND status replies keep answering the
                500 ms poll. `timeouts` and `config_page` are deliberately
                excluded from the verdict: both are dominated by the separate
                open safety_cfg_store_refetch defect (page 0 failed /
                ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT), which climbs identically at every baud rate
                and would fail all of them for a reason that has nothing to
                do with baud.

                  921600 -- PASS. 0 new crc/framing, 0 length mismatch,
                            0 crc mismatch, 0 resync. Status +124 in 62 s
                            (2.00/s).
                  460800 -- PASS. 0 new crc/framing, 0 length mismatch,
                            0 crc mismatch, 0 resync. Status +123 in 62 s
                            (2.00/s).
                  230400 -- PASS. 0 new crc/framing, 0 length mismatch,
                            0 crc mismatch, 0 resync. Status +124 in 62 s
                            (2.01/s).

                Negative control, proving the test can fail: ESP at 230400
                against a Pico deliberately left at 115200 failed in 3 s,
                crc/framing errors rising 941 -> 1062 (+121) and length
                mismatch 58 -> 69.

                CHOSEN VALUE: 230400, i.e. this default. Rule used: the
                fastest passing rate was 921600; one standard step down from
                that is 460800; the chosen value is the slower of that step
                and the requested 230400 -- so 230400, two full standard
                steps below a rate that soaks clean.

                Two methodology traps this sweep ran into, worth remembering
                if it is ever re-run:

                1. `sent` and `received` (from GET_LINK_STATS) are NOT a loss
                   metric and must never be compared to each other. `sent`
                   counts outbound requests of several kinds; `received`
                   counts status replies only, and equals the histogram's
                   `status` count exactly. Comparing them shows ~50% "loss"
                   at every rate, including a deliberately mismatched pair --
                   that is the metric being wrong, not the link.
                2. Flashing resets the Pico while the ESP keeps running, and
                   that reset throws a genuine burst of framing errors (plus
                   `break condition on uart1` / `frame length mismatch` log
                   lines). The measurement window must start after a settle
                   delay following any flash, or every rate fails on reset
                   residue rather than its own behavior -- an earlier sweep
                   attempt misread exactly this residue as a 921600 failure;
                   921600 in fact passes cleanly once measured past the
                   reset.

                Do not raise this above 921600 (the fastest rate measured to
                pass) without re-running the sweep.

                Still true, and still the trap that hid the opto ceiling for
                weeks: static GPIO high/low tests pass at ANY baud rate,
                because both an optocoupler and a digital isolator carry a DC
                level perfectly. A verified-by-GPIO-test wire proves nothing
                about the rate it can carry.

        config KILNCTL_SAFETY_POLL_PERIOD_MS
            int "Safety processor poll period (ms)"
            default 500
            range 0 60000
            help
                How often the firmware asks the safety processor for its
                status. 0 disables polling entirely (the link then only
                carries explicit PING/REQUEST_ENABLE traffic).
    endmenu

    menu "UART Link (PC control interface)"
        choice KILNCTL_UART_PORT_CHOICE
            prompt "UART port"
            default KILNCTL_UART_PORT_0
            help
                Which hardware UART peripheral to use for the PC link.
                UART1 is taken by the safety processor link, so this should
                stay on UART0 unless that link is disabled.

        config KILNCTL_UART_PORT_0
            bool "UART0"
        config KILNCTL_UART_PORT_1
            bool "UART1"
        endchoice

        config KILNCTL_UART_TX_IO
            int "UART TX GPIO"
            default 43
            range 0 48
            help
                GPIO43/44 are the dev board's own UART0 pins, routed through
                its USB-UART bridge and its dedicated USB-C port -- distinct
                from the native USB / USB-Serial-JTAG port (GPIO19/20) used
                for flashing and debugging. The main board leaves both
                unconnected.

        config KILNCTL_UART_RX_IO
            int "UART RX GPIO"
            default 44
            range 0 48

        config KILNCTL_UART_BAUD_RATE
            int "UART baud rate"
            default 921600
            help
                PC link only (UART_OWNER_PORT_NUM), not the isolated safety
                link (KILNCTL_SAFETY_BAUD_RATE, separate UART, unchanged).
                Bumped from 115200 -- the CH343 USB-UART bridge on this board
                is rated well past this, and every frame is still CRC'd and
                retried (protocol.py's Frame/UartLink), so a bad byte at the
                higher rate is a retry, not silent corruption. The dominant
                cost in a DISPLAY blit is still the per-frame stop-and-wait
                ACK round trip (UART_PROTO_MAX_PAYLOAD=128 B/frame, one ACK
                each), so this helps but does not fix that; pipelining
                multiple in-flight frames would need a wire-protocol change,
                not a config one. Must match pc_tools's DEFAULT_BAUD_RATE
                (protocol.py) -- both sides hardcode the same value rather
                than negotiating it.

        config KILNCTL_UART_OWNER_QUEUE_LEN
            int "uart_owner request queue length"
            default 8
            help
                How many in-flight uart_owner_transfer() requests (from any
                caller sharing this owner) can be queued before a caller
                blocks.

        config KILNCTL_UART_OWNER_TASK_PRIORITY
            int "uart_owner task priority"
            default 5

        config KILNCTL_UART_OWNER_STACK_SIZE
            int "uart_owner task stack size (bytes)"
            # 4096 -> 3072 (2026-08-27), on measurement rather than by feel.
            # This one number sizes FOUR tasks: main.c's PC-link uart_owner
            # pair and safety_link.c's safety-link pair. All four are now
            # registered for high-water reporting (they were not before -- the
            # safety pair, the one carrying the telemetry that gates heating,
            # was measured by nobody), and after a firing that ran to
            # completion with rules active the worst of the four had used 904
            # bytes. 3072 leaves 2168 free above that, ~70% headroom.
            #
            # Not cut closer on purpose: a Pico OTA relay transfer streams
            # through the safety-link pair and has never been measured, so the
            # margin has to cover a path the numbers do not yet include. Re-read
            # the high-water marks after a real Pico update before going lower.
            default 3072

        config KILNCTL_UART_PROTOCOL_TASK_PRIORITY
            int "uart_protocol RX task priority"
            default 6

        config KILNCTL_UART_PROTOCOL_STACK_SIZE
            int "uart_protocol RX task stack size (bytes)"
            default 8192
            help
                Raised 6144 -> 8192 on 2026-08-28, second move the same
                day: chunk[] grew again, STUFFED_FRAME_MAX (528) ->
                UART_PROTOCOL_RX_CHUNK_BYTES (2048), at the owner's
                request. That is +1520 bytes of stack, and the whole point
                of the 6144 note below is that the stack must move WITH the
                locals rather than after the crash. 8192 restores roughly
                the ~3 kB free the 6144 bump bought.

                Raised 4096 -> 6144 on 2026-08-28. uart_protocol_rx_task's
                chunk[] buffer grew from 32 bytes to STUFFED_FRAME_MAX
                (~530) to fix a live commissioning defect -- page 1 of a
                CONFIG_PAGE fetch timed out on every attempt because
                assembling it took 5-8 scheduler round trips. That buffer
                plus raw[] (~265) lives on this task's stack, and the stack
                did not move with them: stack_margin then measured
                safety_proto_rx at 1024 B free of 4096 (25%, LOW) -- the
                thinnest margin on the board.

                This is the same shape as the safety_poll crash the same
                day: locals grew, the stack did not, and nothing was
                watching until the task died with IllegalInstruction. Fixed
                before the crash rather than after it.

                NOTE this default is only what a FRESH clone gets --
                sdkconfig is gitignored and wins over it (see the build's
                own "Kconfig defaults policy: use sdkconfig" note), so an
                existing checkout needs its sdkconfig updated too.
    endmenu

